      "content": "No, arguing that a concurrency race condition is a low-probability edge case is generally considered unprofessional and dangerous in this context. Even with caching, race conditions are often the single most common source of authentication failures and security breaches in high-traffic systems, so relying on timing luck is a very risky strategy. You should advise your VP that while a hotfix is better than a hard block, shipping a known race condition often violates security best practices and could expose the company to significant liability, so a brief pause is usually the prudent professional choice.",
